## Solution

**Recognizing the objects.** The elements
$$p_i = \frac{\chi_i(1)}{|G|}\sum_{s\in G}\chi_i(s^{-1})\,s$$
are precisely the **primitive central idempotents** $e_i$ of the semisimple algebra $\mathbb{C}[G]$. They satisfy the well-known relations:
- $e_i^2 = e_i$ (idempotent),
- $e_i e_j = 0$ for $i\neq j$,
- $\sum_{i=1}^h e_i = 1_{\mathbb{C}[G]}$.

**The trace on $\mathbb{C}[G]$.** The trace $\operatorname{Tr}$ is that of the (left) regular representation of $\mathbb{C}[G]$ on itself. For $a=\sum_{s}a_s\,s\in\mathbb{C}[G]$, the left-multiplication map $L_a:x\mapsto ax$ has, on the basis $\{t\}_{t\in G}$, diagonal entry (coefficient of $t$ in $a\,t$) equal to $a_1$ (the coefficient of the identity, since $a\,t=\sum_s a_s\,st$ and the $t$-coefficient comes from $s=1$). Hence
$$\operatorname{Tr}(L_a)=|G|\cdot a_1.$$

**Computing $\operatorname{Tr}(p_i^2)$.** Since $p_i=e_i$ is idempotent, $p_i^2=p_i$, so
$$\operatorname{Tr}(p_i^2)=\operatorname{Tr}(p_i)=|G|\cdot[\text{coefficient of }1\text{ in }p_i].$$
The coefficient of the identity in $p_i$ is
$$\frac{\chi_i(1)}{|G|}\,\chi_i(1^{-1})=\frac{\chi_i(1)}{|G|}\,\chi_i(1)=\frac{\chi_i(1)^2}{|G|}.$$
Therefore
$$\operatorname{Tr}(p_i^2)=|G|\cdot\frac{\chi_i(1)^2}{|G|}=\chi_i(1)^2.$$

**Summing.** Using the fundamental orthogonality relation $\sum_{i=1}^h \chi_i(1)^2=|G|$,
$$\sum_{i=1}^h \operatorname{Tr}(p_i^2)=\sum_{i=1}^h \chi_i(1)^2=|G|.$$

